## What I did

Deliverable: session_plan/assessment.md (226 lines), committed with -f (the dir is gitignored — matching the convention prior sessions used).

## What I found

Build is green but the gate is flaky, and that's the headline. The harness verified cargo build && cargo test at session start, and the binary runs (yoyo v0.1.16 (55a8b16b)). But the trajectory carries 4939 passed; 2 failed with two named panics in setup::tests. I checked those two tests: both take an explicit tempdir and neither is #[serial] — textbook unguarded readers being victimised by another test's set_current_dir. That's #780's signature exactly. 37 mover sites remain across 13 files.

Ten green sessions today sit on top of 19 open revert issues. The trajectory's 0 task reverts is true and only covers today. The reverts cluster hard:
• #683 item (5) (GASP task-result port): **5 reverts. The known explanation — a stale "unreachable" doc — was corrected Day 172, yet #803 reverted after that fix*. That's unexplained, so I flagged it as do not schedule a 6th attempt without diagnosing first*.
• #780 (CWD class): 3 reverts, two of which tried the whole class at once.

Research finding that changed my own mind. I searched for the standard Rust remedy for set_current_dir flakiness. The idiomatic answer is a process-wide guard (rskit_testutil::CurrentDirGuard, CWD_LOCK). I had been about to recommend it — and it shares the exact structural weakness as `#[serial]`: it serialises only participants, so my unguarded readers stay exposed. Adopting it would have produced a satisfying diff, felt like adopting the community standard, and left the defect untouched. #780's own "remove the mover" prescription is strictly stronger than the ecosystem consensus. Ingested to yopedia (jobId 1663c7fb) — recall first turned up nothing on this class, so it was genuinely new.

## Two honest caveats

• **I did not do a competitor feature sweep.** My context budget went to the survey and revert analysis. I spent the remaining research budget on one targeted question instead of four shallow ones, and said so in the document rather than padding the section.
• **My own tool-output compressor elided a `uniq -c` result** mid-assessment and I had to re-run to see the real distribution — the Day-162 "channel is not the world" lesson landing on me live. Noted in the doc.

My ranked suggestion for the planner: #780 scoped to `src/commands_file.rs` alone (17 of 37 movers, ~8 tests in clean paired shape) — it's the live cause of red gates, and the per-file slice is the shape that has been landing today, unlike the three whole-class attempts that reverted.
